  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1114 AM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWV imagery shows one short wave over eastern New Mexico and \ncontinuing to lift northeast, while another short wave just south of \nthe CWA will move into the eastern CWA this afternoon. This feature \ncould allow a few thunderstorms to develop, mostly over Otero and \nHudspeth Counties this afternoon and evening. Moisture and \ninstability still look respectable, so a few of these storms could \napproach severe (mainly for hail). \n\nLooks like we should finally get a brief break from the rain and \nstorms for Tuesday and Wednesday as west flow and drier air set up \nacross the area south of the upper low cutting through the Pac NW \nand northern Rockies. Temperatures should remain just above normal. \n\nThursday into the weekend...sub-tropical jet becomes more active and \nbrings several impulses across the eastern Pacific to northern \nMexico and our CWA. This will turn upper flow more southwest while \nmid-level flow turns more southerly and begins bringing more sub-\ntropical moisture back to the area. One impulse moves up to the CWA \nThursday afternoon/evening to return a chance of \nshowers/thunderstorms to the area. Another short wave moves in \nFriday for another chance of rain late in the day. Saturday may be \ndry as it looks like the day in-between systems. A better defined \ntrough moves toward the area Sunday reaching SoCal by Sunday \nafternoon with a chance of rain in the west late in the day. This \nperiod with the showers/thunderstorms looks to have less potential \nfor severe weather than we have experienced the past several days. \nGFS shows only very modest CAPE values and shear environment looks \nrelatively weak. \n\nThe trough moves through the area Monday for area wide chances of \nrain, with models then showing a longer period of dry weather after \nMonday.",
